    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">DSCR Loans for Foreign Nationals: How the Property Qualifies When You Can't Document US Income</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you live outside the United States and want to purchase a rental property here as an investment, the documentation challenge of a standard foreign national loan (foreign income verification, translated tax returns, home country credit reports) can feel like a significant obstacle. A DSCR loan removes most of that obstacle. Because a DSCR loan qualifies the property on its rental income rather than the borrower on their personal income, much of the documentation complexity that applies to owner-occupied foreign national loans simply doesn't apply.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why DSCR Works Particularly Well for Foreign National Investors</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A Debt Service Coverage Ratio loan evaluates whether the property's rental income covers the mortgage payment at a sufficient ratio. The borrower's personal income, their tax returns, their employment history: none of it enters the core qualification calculation. For a foreign national investor whose income documentation exists in another country, another currency, and another language, that distinction is significant. The property either cash flows at the required ratio or it doesn't, and the lender's primary underwriting question is about the asset, not the person buying it.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why DSCR Requires Fewer Documents Than a Standard Foreign National Loan</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A standard foreign national loan requires income documentation from your home country, which means translated tax returns, employer letters or business financials, and a credit report from a foreign bureau. A DSCR loan for a foreign national investor typically requires a valid passport, proof of funds for the down payment and reserves, and documentation that the property generates sufficient rental income to meet the coverage ratio.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Some lenders do require a credit report from the borrower's home country even for DSCR loans, and credit score requirements vary. The overall documentation profile is cleaner and more manageable than a full income-documented foreign national loan.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How the DSCR Calculation Works for Investment Property</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">DSCR is calculated by dividing the property's monthly gross rental income by its total monthly payment including principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and any HOA dues. A ratio of 1.0 means the rental income exactly covers the payment. Most lenders require a ratio of 1.10 to 1.25 for standard pricing. Properties with ratios below that threshold may still qualify at adjusted pricing or with a larger down payment.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For foreign national borrowers specifically, down payment requirements on DSCR loans are typically higher than for US-based borrowers, often 30 to 40% depending on the lender and the property. That higher equity position reduces the lender's risk in the absence of full US borrower documentation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Identify the target property and calculate its DSCR before you choose a lender.</strong> Divide the monthly market rent by the estimated total monthly payment at current rates. If the ratio is 1.10 or higher, standard DSCR pricing is likely available. If it's below that, understand how the lender handles lower-ratio properties before you proceed.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm your down payment capacity.</strong> Foreign national DSCR loans typically require 30 to 40% down. Know that number before you finalize a purchase price.</li>
      <li><strong>Document your down payment funds clearly.</strong> As with all foreign national programs, lenders will want to see the source and history of the funds you're bringing to closing. Bank statements showing the funds held over a period of time are the standard requirement.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask lenders specifically whether they offer DSCR loans to foreign nationals.</strong> Not all DSCR lenders extend the product to international buyers. Finding one who does before you invest time in the process is the right first step.</li>
      <li><strong>Understand the reserve requirement.</strong> DSCR lenders typically require several months of total monthly payments held in reserves after closing. For foreign national borrowers, that requirement is often higher. Factor that into your total capital planning before you commit to a purchase price.</li>
    </ol>

